file: serve pre-compressed .gz siblings

-z: when the client accepts gzip and file.gz is a readable regular file
at least as new as file, send it with Content-Encoding: gzip. ETag,
Last-Modified, Content-Length and conditional requests use the .gz;
Content-Type comes from the original. A stale .gz is ignored, and any
file with a fresh .gz sibling gets Vary: Accept-Encoding.

Accept-Encoding is parsed per RFC 9110 12.5.3: codings are matched
case-insensitively (gzip or x-gzip), whitespace around ";" is allowed,
q-values are parsed as numbers (q=0, q=0.0, q=0.000 refuse), and a list
that does not name gzip falls back to "*". A malformed q counts as q=0,
so a refusal is never misread as acceptance.

uhttpd has no compression of its own. This lets a build ship gzipped
text assets, at the cost of the .gz copies in flash.

Applies on top of 0008 (Cache-Control).

